#pagebreak(weak: true)
= Abstract

The tropical rainforest of Tsiazombazaha (640~ha), the largest natural forest fragment in the Fitovinany region (Madagascar), is under community management by the VOI Maharitra. This diagnosis describes the forest's conservation status, the pressures it faces and its management, in order to guide the strategy of Inter Aide. It combines an inventory of 1,999 stems in 28 plots, 34 interviews and 21 field outings. The forest is rich but disturbed. Its flora includes 303 vernacular names; its basal area (37.3~±~5.3~m²/ha) is typical of tropical rainforests, but small stems dominate and large trees are rare; it stores 175~tC/ha (95~% CI: [135; 223]). After a sharp decline until 1990, its area has stabilised. Residents see it first as a source of water, then of wood, and attribute its degradation to fires, hunger and lack of land. The VOI's management, still fragile, has been consolidating since the renewal of the management transfer in 2025, but still depends on external funding. This report draws recommendations for Inter Aide from these findings.

*Keywords:* Madagascar, tropical rainforest, socio-ecology, ecological inventory, social survey, community management, management transfer

